title: AWS vs Azure vs GCP Security
page: cloud-security-comparison.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: The native security capabilities of AWS, Azure and GCP are roughly 80% equivalent in 2026; the 20% delta is where the choice matters.

== The 30-second version
layout: compare
section: top
col: GCP leads on | VPC Service Controls | The BeyondCorp / Zero Trust story
col: Azure leads on | Entra ID | Defender for Cloud multi-cloud reach | Microsoft-stack integration
col: AWS leads on | Service breadth | Regional depth | The longest-running operational playbooks
The native security capabilities of AWS, Azure and GCP are roughly 80% equivalent in 2026. The 20% delta is where the choice matters.
notes:
Each provider ships identity, posture management, key management, audit logging, WAF, DDoS protection and dozens of compliance attestations. That is why the page calls the native capabilities of the three roughly 80% equivalent in 2026.

The 20% delta is where the choice matters. GCP leads on VPC Service Controls and the BeyondCorp / Zero Trust story. Azure leads on Entra ID, Defender for Cloud's multi-cloud reach and Microsoft-stack integration. AWS leads on service breadth, regional depth and the longest-running operational playbooks.

The page's advice: choose the cloud, don't fight it. Where you must run all three, run a third-party CNAPP over the top to normalize the view. The tables in this deck are excerpts meant to be skimmed; the conceptual slides cover the differences that don't fit in a grid.

== Choose the cloud, don't fight it
layout: list
section: intro
The most expensive mistake in cloud security is treating the cloud you're on as if it were one of the others.
item: AWS teams try to write JSON allow/deny policies against Azure RBAC
item: Azure teams look in GCP for management groups that aren't there
item: GCP teams re-implement VPC Service Controls on AWS, in flawed software
notes:
The page opens with the most expensive mistake in cloud security: treating the cloud you're on as if it were one of the others.

Teams that came up on AWS look at Azure RBAC and try to write JSON allow and deny policies against it. Teams from Azure land in GCP and look for management groups that aren't there. GCP-native teams move to AWS and miss VPC Service Controls so badly they re-implement it in flawed software.

Each cloud has a coherent security model, and the model is internally consistent. The cost of fighting it is paid in unintended permissions, misconfigurations the CSPM doesn't flag, and architectures the auditor doesn't recognize.

== Parity is high; pricing differs most
layout: text
section: intro
A capability that's free on one cloud and metered on another can quietly drive a six-figure spend variance with no functional difference.
- Capability parity is high
- Operational ergonomics differ
- The integration story differs more
- The pricing model differs the most
notes:
The page is written for three readers. You've already picked your cloud, or had it picked for you, and want to know concretely what the security delta is to the others. Or you're picking between them and want a practitioner's read rather than the marketing matrix. Or you're running on more than one and want to understand the gaps between the native tools well enough to fill them with a CNAPP or a thin internal abstraction.

The framing for everything that follows: capability parity is high, operational ergonomics differ, the integration story differs more, and the pricing model differs the most. A capability that's free on one cloud and metered on another can quietly drive a six-figure spend variance with no functional difference. That is why the page gives pricing disproportionate attention.

== Quick verdict by category
layout: table
section: verdict
row: Category | Where the page puts each lead (opinionated, contestable)
row: Identity | AWS on policy expressiveness, Azure on governance, GCP on workload identity
row: Detection | Azure and GCP tie on integrated CNAPP; AWS leads on aggregation breadth
row: Data | Tie on KMS, HSM, BYOK, confidential compute; GCP on DLP, Azure on classification
row: Network | GCP leads; AWS and Azure match most of it, but compose less elegantly
row: Compliance | AWS on breadth, Azure on Microsoft-stack, GCP on Zero Trust frameworks
row: Containers | GCP on GKE, Azure on registry scanning depth, AWS on ecosystem
For most commercial workloads, all three clear the compliance bar.
notes:
Skim this once, then go to the relevant table. The page says every line is opinionated and contestable.

Identity: AWS leads on policy expressiveness, because IAM JSON conditions are the richest. Azure leads on identity governance, with Entra ID PIM, Access Reviews and Conditional Access. GCP leads on workload identity. Detection: Defender for Cloud and Security Command Center Premium are both mature and unified, so Azure and GCP tie; AWS leads on aggregation breadth, since Security Hub pulls from a dozen specialist services. Data protection is a tie on KMS, HSM, BYOK and confidential compute, with GCP leading on DLP and Azure on data classification through Purview. On network security GCP leads; AWS and Azure both shipped equivalents to most of its network primitives, but they don't compose as elegantly. On compliance, AWS leads on breadth, Azure on Microsoft-stack regulated workloads and GCP on Zero-Trust-shaped frameworks; for most commercial workloads, all three clear the bar.

The page also gives verdicts this table leaves out. Azure leads on customer identity with Entra External ID. AWS leads on compute hardening defaults since IMDSv2-only became the console Quick Start default and an account-level option. On serverless, AWS leads on maturity, Cloud Run on ergonomics and Azure Functions on Microsoft-stack integration. On pricing, the page's verdict is that it's complicated.

== Identity and access, side by side
layout: table
section: tables
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Policy language | JSON Allow / Deny; the richest conditions | RBAC roles assigned at a scope | Inherited bindings, plus IAM Conditions
row: Just-in-time elevation | Temporary credentials; no time-bound activation workflow like PIM | Entra ID PIM: the deepest of the three | Privileged Access Manager
row: Workload identity | IAM roles; IRSA or pod identity on EKS | Managed identities | Service accounts: the cleanest model
row: Identity governance | Limited native IGA | Entra ID Governance: the most complete | Less complete than Entra
row: Preventive policy | SCPs and RCPs | Azure Policy deny effect | Organization Policy constraints
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's identity table.

AWS policies are JSON Allow and Deny statements with Action, Resource and Condition, the richest condition language of the three. Azure assigns built-in or custom RBAC roles at a management group, subscription, resource group or resource. GCP binds members to roles on resources, with inheritance down the org hierarchy, and IAM Conditions add expression-based constraints.

For just-in-time elevation, Entra ID Privileged Identity Management gives time-bound, approval-gated role activation, the deepest of the three. AWS has IAM Identity Center temporary credentials, session policies and permission sets, but no built-in time-bound role-activation workflow comparable to PIM. GCP's Privileged Access Manager has been generally available since 16 September 2024; newer features such as multi-party approval remain in preview.

For least privilege, AWS has IAM Access Analyzer, and GCP has IAM Recommender and Policy Analyzer. On Azure, CIEM sits in Defender for Cloud's Defender CSPM plan, and the standalone Entra Permissions Management was retired on 1 November 2025.

Trust between accounts differs too. AWS uses role assumption with explicit trust policies. Azure trusts implicitly within a tenant. In GCP, the org hierarchy makes most cross-project access a single grant.

== Detection and response, side by side
layout: table
section: tables
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Native CSPM | Security Hub CSPM and specialist services | Defender for Cloud: full, multi-cloud | SCC: full CSPM at Premium
row: SIEM | Security Lake; no native SIEM SaaS | Microsoft Sentinel | Google Security Operations
row: On by default | CloudTrail management events | Activity Log | Admin Activity, always on and free
row: Free log retention | 90 days in the console | 90 days | Admin Activity: 400 days
row: Cross-cloud detection | Partner integrations; Security Lake OCSF | Native connectors to AWS and GCP | Enterprise tier only, ending 21 May 2027
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's detection table.

On AWS, Security Hub CSPM aggregates posture checks, and Security Hub correlates the findings of Config Rules, Inspector, IAM Access Analyzer and Macie into exposure findings. Defender for Cloud is a full, integrated, multi-cloud CSPM. Security Command Center gives full CSPM at Premium; its Enterprise tier, the multi-cloud CNAPP tier, shuts down on 21 May 2027.

AWS has no native SIEM SaaS: Amazon Security Lake stores logs in the OCSF schema, queryable from Athena or partner SIEMs. Microsoft Sentinel is a full SaaS SIEM and SOAR with KQL. Google Security Operations, formerly Chronicle, is a full SaaS SIEM with petabyte ingest and YARA-L rules.

CloudTrail management events are on by default in every account; data events are opt-in and the most expensive log class. Azure's Activity Log is on by default, but Diagnostic Settings are opt-in per resource. GCP's Admin Activity logs are always on and free, and Data Access logs are opt-in except for BigQuery.

For cross-cloud detection, the page calls Defender for Cloud's native connectors to AWS and GCP the strongest first-party multi-cloud story. SCC Premium is Google Cloud only.

== Data protection, side by side
layout: table
section: tables
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: KMS | AWS KMS, per-region | Key Vault; HSM-backed in Premium | Cloud KMS: HSM or software
row: HSM | CloudHSM, separate from KMS | Managed HSM, integrated with KMS | Cloud HSM, a KMS protection level
row: Hold-your-own-key | External Key Store (XKS) | Managed HSM external key management | External Key Manager: the most mature
row: DLP | Macie, S3-focused | Purview Information Protection | Sensitive Data Protection: most accurate detectors
row: Encryption in use / tokenization | Lambda and KMS pattern | Always Encrypted (SQL) | The only native tokenization service
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's data protection table.

All three offer customer-managed keys, BYOK and confidential compute. The HSMs differ in how they attach: CloudHSM is a dedicated FIPS 140-2 Level 3 cluster, separate from KMS. Azure Key Vault Managed HSM is FIPS 140-3 Level 3 and integrated with the KMS abstraction. Cloud HSM is FIPS 140-2 Level 3 and integrated into Cloud KMS as a protection level.

For keys held outside the provider, AWS KMS External Key Store calls out to keys that live outside AWS. Azure's Managed HSM external key management keeps the key encryption key in an HSM you operate outside Microsoft infrastructure. The page calls GCP's External Key Manager the most mature of the three; with VPC Service Controls it keeps both the keys and the data path under your control.

For DLP, Macie is S3-focused and classifier-based. Purview Information Protection is broad, covering M365, files and databases. Sensitive Data Protection covers BigQuery, GCS, Datastore and arbitrary text, and its de-identification (format-preserving encryption, tokenization, masking, bucketing) is the only native tokenization service of the three. Macie classifies but doesn't tokenize.

== Network security, side by side
layout: table
section: tables
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: WAF | AWS WAF; managed and custom rules | Azure WAF; Microsoft and OWASP rules | Cloud Armor: the most flexible rule language
row: Free DDoS | Shield Standard | Infrastructure protection | Cloud Armor always-on baseline
row: Private endpoints | VPC Endpoints (PrivateLink) | Private Endpoint: broader coverage | Private Service Connect: the most flexible
row: VPC isolation | Regional VPC; Transit Gateway | Regional VNet; Virtual WAN | Global VPC by default; Shared VPC
row: Managed service mesh | None since App Mesh retired | Istio-based add-on for AKS | Cloud Service Mesh, managed Istio
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's network table.

Every cloud has a free DDoS baseline. The paid tiers are AWS Shield Advanced, at $3,000 a month per payer account on a 1-year commitment plus data-transfer fees; Azure DDoS Network Protection, a fixed monthly plan covering 100 public IPs; and Cloud Armor Enterprise, with ML-based adaptive protection.

The network firewalls line up as Security Groups, NACLs and AWS Network Firewall on AWS; NSGs, Azure Firewall and Firewall Manager on Azure; and VPC firewall rules with Cloud NGFW on GCP.

AWS retired App Mesh on 30 September 2026. Its migration paths are ECS Service Connect and Amazon VPC Lattice, or self-managed Istio or Linkerd on EKS. On AKS, the Open Service Mesh add-on is supported only until 30 September 2027.

GCP's VPC is global by default, with Shared VPC across projects, and the page calls it the cleanest of the three at multi-region scale. For network telemetry, AWS has VPC Flow Logs and Traffic Mirroring; Azure has NSG Flow Logs and Network Watcher; GCP has VPC Flow Logs, Packet Mirroring, Firewall Insights and Network Intelligence Center.

== Compliance, side by side
layout: table
section: tables
row: Framework | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: FedRAMP High | GovCloud (US) and select commercial-region services | Azure Government | Assured Workloads: IL2 / IL4 / IL5
row: DoD IL5 / IL6 | GovCloud; Secret Region for IL6 | Azure Government Secret / Top Secret | IL5 (growing); IL6 limited
row: HIPAA | ~150+ eligible services | Comparable coverage with BAA | ~100+ HIPAA-covered services
row: PCI DSS 4.0 | Service Provider Level 1 | Service Provider Level 1 | Service Provider Level 1
row: EU sovereign cloud | European Sovereign Cloud, generally available since January 2026 | Microsoft Sovereign Cloud, EU Data Boundary | Google Cloud Data Boundary
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's compliance table.

For FedRAMP Moderate, AWS covers most US regions with the deepest catalog of in-scope services, Azure Commercial covers a subset of services, and GCP covers it through Assured Workloads US Regions. For FedRAMP High and DoD work, AWS has GovCloud and the Secret Region, which the page calls the deepest DoD coverage. Azure has Azure Government and its Secret and Top Secret clouds. GCP's Assured Workloads reach IL5, which the page calls growing, with IL6 limited.

On PCI DSS 4.0 all three hold Service Provider Level 1 with full attestation. All three are certified to ISO 27001, 27017 and 27018 across most regions, and all three publish SOC 2 Type 2 reports annually.

For EU sovereignty, the AWS European Sovereign Cloud has been generally available since January 2026, with its first Region in Brandenburg, Germany. Microsoft offers Microsoft Sovereign Cloud and the EU Data Boundary. Google offers Google Cloud Data Boundary, including Data Boundary by Partners: T-Systems in Germany, S3NS in France and Polo Strategico Nazionale in Italy.

For continuous compliance, the dashboards are Security Hub with AWS Audit Manager, Defender for Cloud's regulatory-compliance dashboard with Purview Compliance Manager, and Security Command Center's compliance posture with Assured Workloads attestations.

== Pricing: where the clouds differ most
layout: table
section: tables
row: Pricing | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: KMS per key | $1 / month per CMK | No per-key fee for software keys | $0.06 / month per active software key version
row: KMS per request | $0.03 per 10,000 requests | $0.03 per 10,000 transactions | $0.03 per 10,000 symmetric operations
row: Audit log retention | 90 days free; S3 / Glacier cheap | Activity Log 90 days free | Admin Activity 400 days free
row: Egress to internet | Typically $0.09 / GB at low volume | Typically $0.087 / GB | Typically $0.12 / GB (premium tier)
Functional capability is similar. Pricing structure can swing a workload's annual security bill by 5-10×.
notes:
The page calls pricing the line item where the clouds differ most. Functional capability is similar, but pricing structure can swing a workload's annual security bill by 5-10×.

Threat detection is priced differently on each. GuardDuty is priced per event plus per protected workload, with features such as Malware Protection and Runtime billed separately. Defender plans are priced per resource per month, each workload type its own SKU. SCC Premium and Enterprise are priced per asset or per workload. For CSPM, Security Hub charges per check and per finding ingested; Defender for Cloud includes free Foundational CSPM; SCC Standard is free for basic posture.

Per key, GCP charges $0.06 a month per active software key version against $1 a month per AWS CMK, and the page calls GCP cheaper than AWS once those savings are included. Azure charges no per-key fee for software keys. The request price is $0.03 per 10,000 on all three.

Egress is tiered on every cloud, and these are the page's typical rates to the internet; the AWS and GCP rates are at low volume. The page says egress dominates almost every multi-cloud bill. The fastest way to lose money in cloud security is to architect cross-cloud data flows where logs, backups or threat-intel feeds traverse provider boundaries hot. Keep telemetry where the workload runs; export aggregates, not raw events.

== Compute, containers and serverless
layout: table
section: tables
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Metadata service | IMDSv2; IMDSv2-only default for some launches | Metadata: true header required | Metadata-Flavor: Google header required
row: Secure boot | UEFI Secure Boot on supported families | Trusted Launch, default for Gen 2 | Shielded VMs, default for most images
row: Managed Kubernetes | EKS, Fargate or EKS Auto Mode | AKS | GKE Autopilot: the most opinionated
row: Signed images | Third-party admission; Signer for Lambda | Cosign, Notation; Defender policy | Binary Authorization: the most mature
row: Serverless isolation | Firecracker microVMs | Hyper-V isolation on Premium | gVisor or a microVM
notes:
This is an excerpt of three of the page's tables: compute, containers and serverless.

On AWS, IMDSv2-only is the default for console Quick Start launches and IMDSv2-flagged AMIs such as Amazon Linux 2023, and an opt-in account-level default since 2024, but not for every new instance. Azure and GCP require a header on every metadata request and have no hop-limit by default.

The default networking missteps differ: overly broad security group rules on EC2, RDP or SSH open to the internet on Azure, and the default-allow-ssh firewall rules on Compute Engine's default network.

For containers, Defender for Containers has the deepest registry-side reach. GKE Workload Identity is the cleanest of the three workload identity models in Kubernetes, and Binary Authorization is native, declarative and attestation-based.

For serverless, each platform gives a function its own identity: an execution role on Lambda, a managed identity on Azure Functions, and a service account on Cloud Run functions. AWS Signer for Lambda enforces a signed code policy; Azure has no native Functions-level enforcement; Binary Authorization covers Cloud Run. On GCP, the first-generation execution environment uses gVisor and the second a microVM.

== Three IAM policy models
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: AWS | JSON policies on identities and resources | An explicit Deny anywhere wins | Enormous expressiveness, at the cost of complexity
col: Azure | A role, assigned to a principal, at a scope | No resource-side policies | Azure Policy governs resource configurations
col: GCP | Member, role and resource bindings | Bindings inherit down the org hierarchy | Every principal in one namespace
On AWS, SCPs only restrict and IAM only grants. There is no single inheritance tree, which surprises engineers trained on GCP or Azure.
notes:
AWS uses an identity-and-resource policy model. Both identities and resources such as S3 buckets and KMS keys can carry JSON policies, the evaluation engine runs every applicable policy, and an explicit Deny anywhere wins. You can pin a permission to a resource tag, the time of day, the source IP or the MFA state of the session. The cost is complexity: nobody fully understands how cross-account access composes with SCPs, RCPs and permission boundaries without a few hours with the evaluation flowchart.

Azure uses RBAC: a role is assigned to a principal at a management group, subscription, resource group or resource, and inheritance is straightforward. Role assignment conditions exist but apply only to Blob and Queue Storage data actions and to delegated role assignment. Azure Policy layers audit, deny and append effects on resource configurations, for shapes of resources rather than granted permissions.

GCP binds members to roles anywhere in the org hierarchy, and bindings inherit downward. IAM Conditions add a CEL-expression layer. Workloads, users, groups and service accounts are all principals in the same namespace.

The practical impact: an AWS-trained engineer reaches for a JSON policy on a GCP resource and finds the model rejects the framing. GCP and Azure engineers expect inheritance to cascade and find that on AWS, SCPs only restrict and IAM only grants.

== The unit of isolation
layout: figure
section: concepts
figure: isolation-unit
AWS forces stronger boundaries by default. GCP makes cross-project access trivially easy, sometimes too easy. Azure sits between.
notes:
The figure ranks the unit of isolation on each cloud from the strongest boundary to the lightest: the AWS account, the Azure subscription and the GCP project.

An AWS account has separate IAM, separate billing, separate API endpoints and separate VPCs. AWS Organizations groups accounts into OUs with SCPs and RCPs, and landing zones built with Control Tower typically give each workload, or group of related workloads, separate accounts per environment.

An Azure subscription is a lighter boundary: subscriptions share the tenant and its identity plane, with billing separation. Management Groups group subscriptions, and Azure Policy and RBAC inherit through the tree.

A GCP project is the lightest of the three. Projects share an organization, identity and usually Shared VPC, under an org, folders and projects hierarchy.

The implication: on AWS the account is the answer to most blast radius questions. On GCP, a misclick in IAM at the org level can grant something across every project.

== Log retention: the line item that surprises
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: AWS | CloudTrail management events free for 90 days | Cheap to keep forever in S3 and Glacier
col: Azure | Activity Log kept free for 90 days | Per-GB ingestion cost comes from resource logs
col: GCP | Admin Activity kept free for 400 days | Sinks to BigQuery / GCS are cheap
If you treat all three the same, with verbose logs everywhere kept 7 years, Azure can cost much more on logging. Budget log ingestion per cloud.
notes:
The page says this line item surprises every newly multi-cloud team.

Most AWS-native shops default to all CloudTrail to S3 forever, partitioned by date and account. Azure's Activity Log keeps 90 days free; for longer, a diagnostic setting sends it to a Storage account or a Log Analytics workspace. Log Analytics charges no ingestion for Activity Log and bills only retention past 90 days; the per-GB ingestion cost comes from the resource logs that verbose Diagnostic Settings add. GCP retains Admin Activity for 400 days free, and Data Access logs are opt-in except for BigQuery.

If you treat all three the same, with verbose logs everywhere retained 7 years, Azure can cost much more than the others on logging.

The practical advice: budget log ingestion explicitly per cloud. Cap Log Analytics ingest, route the firehose to cheap storage, and reserve premium ingest for the signals your detection rules use.

== VPC Service Controls, GCP's killer feature
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: GCP | VPC Service Controls | One perimeter around projects and resources
col: AWS | Nothing comparable as one construct | Assembled from SCPs, RCPs, endpoint and resource policies
col: Azure | Network Security Perimeter | Only onboarded services; some still in preview
A perimeter denies a request from outside it even when IAM allows it. IAM says yes, the perimeter says yes, and only then does the call succeed.
notes:
VPC Service Controls establish a service perimeter around a set of projects and resources, blocking traffic across the perimeter even for fully authorized identities. If your IAM is misconfigured and a foreign service account has Storage Object Viewer on your bucket, the perimeter still denies the access if the request didn't originate inside it. It's a second factor for the resource access decision.

AWS has nothing comparable as a unified construct. The closest analog combines SCPs with aws:SourceVpce conditions, resource control policies, VPC endpoint policies, resource policies and S3 access points. It's workable, but you assemble it from several primitives and re-verify the composition each time.

Azure has come closer. Network Security Perimeter puts PaaS resources such as Storage, Key Vault and Event Hubs inside one perimeter with shared access rules, though only onboarded services can join and several, such as Cosmos DB and SQL Database, are still in preview.

For regulated workloads where data exfiltration is the headline risk, such as PHI, classified data or financial PII, the page calls this one of the biggest reasons teams pick GCP.

== Private endpoints: what gets privatized
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: AWS PrivateLink | Per-service, per-region | An ENI in your VPC | S3 and DynamoDB gateway endpoints: a separate, free pattern
col: Azure Private Endpoint | Per-resource | A NIC with a private IP | Almost every PaaS service supports it
col: GCP Private Service Connect | Per-published-target | Endpoints, backends and producers | The most flexible of the three
All three offer a private endpoint for a managed service. When you make it private, the granularity of what's privatized differs.
notes:
All three clouds offer a private endpoint for a managed service. They look similar, but they aren't quite the same.

An AWS Interface VPC Endpoint gives you an ENI in your VPC that resolves to a regional managed service, per service and per region. Gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB are a separate, free, route-table-based pattern.

Azure Private Endpoint gives you a NIC in your VNet with a private IP for a specific resource, not just a service. Almost every PaaS service supports it, and Private Link Service lets you publish your own service to other tenants.

GCP Private Service Connect is the most flexible of the three. Endpoints connect to Google APIs or to third-party and first-party producers, backends extend regional managed services into your VPC, and you can publish your own services as producers.

Pricing differs in subtle ways too: per endpoint-hour plus data processing on AWS, per endpoint plus per GB on Azure, per endpoint plus traffic on GCP.

== Sprawl and auditors differ too
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: Account and project sprawl | AWS: dozens to hundreds of accounts | GCP: dozens to hundreds of projects | Azure: dozens of subscriptions, many resource groups | Build the topology for the cloud you're on
col: Auditor familiarity | North American SOC 2 auditors find AWS evidence familiar | Their GCP fluency is sometimes weaker | EU auditors increasingly fluent in Azure and AWS | 3PAOs: deepest FedRAMP experience with AWS GovCloud
notes:
The right level of sprawl differs by cloud. An idiomatic AWS landing zone has dozens to hundreds of accounts, an idiomatic GCP environment dozens to hundreds of projects, and an idiomatic Azure environment dozens of subscriptions with many more resource groups inside each. Moving an AWS-shaped account topology onto Azure produces too many subscriptions; moving an Azure-shaped subscription model onto AWS produces too few accounts. Don't replicate the wrong granularity.

The page calls auditor familiarity an under-discussed delta. North American SOC 2 auditors have run thousands of AWS audits and find its evidence formats familiar; their Azure muscle memory is solid, and their GCP fluency is sometimes weaker. EU auditors are increasingly fluent in Azure and AWS. For FedRAMP, 3PAOs have the deepest experience with AWS GovCloud, growing experience with Azure Government, and the newest experience with GCP Assured Workloads. The cost differential of an auditor's first GCP engagement against their hundredth AWS one is real.

== Which cloud should you pick for…
layout: table
section: pick
row: Scenario | The page's pick
row: US federal, regulated | AWS GovCloud; GCP Assured Workloads for newer ones
row: Microsoft-ecosystem shops | Azure
row: Most aggressive Zero Trust | GCP, with Azure a strong second
row: Largest talent pool | AWS, by a wide margin
row: Greenfield Kubernetes | GKE Autopilot; EKS Auto Mode closes much of the gap
row: Cheapest log retention at scale | AWS, generally
Opinionated guidance. Disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz, which Google owns.
notes:
This is opinionated guidance for the cases where one provider is clearly differentiated. Where two clouds are close, the tables are the better tool. The page discloses that the site's author works at Wiz, which Google owns; the acquisition completed on 11 March 2026.

For US federal work, AWS GovCloud fits FedRAMP High and DoD IL5+ where the longest catalog of in-scope services decides it, and GCP Assured Workloads fits newer workloads that benefit from policy-driven boundary enforcement and VPC Service Controls. Azure Government is a close third, and the obvious pick if the rest of the agency runs on Microsoft. For Microsoft-ecosystem shops, the Entra ID, Microsoft 365, Defender XDR, Sentinel and Intune story is the most coherent identity-to-endpoint-to-cloud telemetry pipeline of the three.

GCP's BeyondCorp heritage makes it the page's pick for Zero Trust. If hiring is a constraint, the page says to optimize for the talent market, even if the technical fit is closer to another cloud. AKS is the most flexible Kubernetes option but needs the most explicit hardening up front.

The page hedges its other three picks. Detection-engineering ergonomics depend on the team: GCP for a team that loves Google SecOps, AWS for a SIEM-agnostic approach, Azure for a team already in Sentinel. Native CSPM and CNAPP maturity is an effective tie between Defender for Cloud and SCC Premium, though SCC's AWS and Azure coverage sits in the Enterprise tier Google shuts down on 21 May 2027. For AI and ML security the page sees a trend toward GCP and Azure, with AWS Bedrock and Guardrails a strong third.

== The third-party normalizer
layout: table
section: normalizer
row: Layer | What one CNAPP normalizes across clouds
row: Asset inventory | Every VM, image, identity, secret and datastore in one graph
row: Risk model | Cross-cloud attack paths the native tools can't see
row: Policy library | One CIS / NIST / SOC 2 / PCI pack for all three
row: Detection | Runtime or agentless, whichever cloud the workload runs on
row: Workflow | One ticketing flow, severity rubric and on-call rotation
Each native tool produces findings in its own cloud's idiom. The native tools don't see across each other.
notes:
The longer an organization runs on more than one cloud, the more attractive a single CNAPP across all of them becomes. The page's earlier sections argue the native tools are good, but each produces findings in its own cloud's idiom: GuardDuty in CloudTrail terms, Defender for Cloud in ARM-resource terms, SCC in GCP-asset terms. Cross-cloud correlation, cross-cloud risk ranking and a single dashboard for one incident-response team are work each native tool only does well for its own cloud.

Take the risk model: a CNAPP can combine an exposed asset on AWS with an over-permissioned identity on Azure that has access to a vulnerable workload on GCP into a single attack-path finding. The native tools don't see across each other.

The page lists the major options in 2026 as Wiz, Orca Security, FortiCNAPP, Sysdig, Cortex Cloud, CrowdStrike Falcon Cloud Security, Check Point CloudGuard and others (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz). Microsoft Defender for Cloud is also a credible cross-cloud choice for Azure-heavy organizations. None of these is a substitute for the native primitives; they sit above them, querying APIs and consuming logs. The page asks you to calibrate its characterization against your own evaluation.

== The hybrid pattern for most multi-cloud orgs
layout: layers
section: normalizer
layer: Hunting and audit | Cloud-agnostic SIEM | GRC platform
layer: System of record | Third-party CNAPP
layer: Per-cloud signal | Native detection
layer: Enforcement | Native SCPs, Azure Policy, Org Policy and IAM
It costs more than going all-in on one cloud's native tooling. It's also the only way a 4-person security team operates 800 cloud accounts across three providers without losing sleep.
notes:
The page describes the hybrid pattern that works for most multi-cloud organizations, from the bottom up.

Native tools are the enforcement plane: SCPs, Azure Policy, Org Policy and native IAM, so the control runs where the resource runs. Native detection, such as GuardDuty, Defender and SCC threat detection, is the per-cloud signal source, because the cloud sees its own anomalies first. A third-party CNAPP is the system of record for posture, asset inventory, risk ranking and the cross-cloud attack-path view. A cloud-agnostic SIEM, such as Splunk, Panther, Elastic, Sumo, Google SecOps or Sentinel, is the investigation and hunting plane. A GRC platform, such as Vanta, Drata or ServiceNow GRC, is the auditor-facing layer.

The page says this stack is more expensive than going all-in on one cloud's native tooling, and says it's also the only way a 4-person security team operates 800 cloud accounts across three providers without losing sleep.

== Scorecard excerpt
layout: table
section: scorecard
row: Category | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Identity (IAM expressiveness) | Leader | Solid | Solid
row: Identity governance (IGA / PIM) | Catching up | Leader | Solid
row: CSPM / CNAPP (native) | Solid | Leader | Leader
row: VPC perimeter (data-exfil prevention) | Catching up | Catching up | Leader
row: Container security (managed K8s) | Solid | Solid | Leader
row: Talent market | Leader | Solid | Catching up
Leader: meaningfully ahead for most teams. Solid: production-grade; the choice rarely hinges on it. Catching up: behind today, on a credible roadmap.
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's scorecard, which gives every category a one-word verdict per cloud as an executive-friendly one-pager.

Leader means the provider is meaningfully ahead in the category for most teams in 2026. Solid means a strong, production-grade offering, close enough that the choice rarely hinges on it. Catching up means behind the leaders today but on a credible roadmap.

The rows the slide leaves out: AWS leads on serverless security maturity, log retention pricing and service breadth. GCP leads on workload identity, audit logging defaults, DLP, private connectivity and Zero Trust heritage. Azure leads on Microsoft-stack integration. Azure and GCP both lead on native SIEM. AWS and Azure both lead on compliance breadth and on FedRAMP High and DoD IL5+, where GCP is catching up. AWS is also catching up on native SIEM, DLP, Zero Trust heritage and Microsoft-stack integration, GCP on Microsoft-stack integration, and Azure on log retention pricing. On KMS and HSM, and on WAF and DDoS, the page rates all three Solid.

The page says every row is contestable; the goal is a defensible starting point, not the final word.

== Which cloud is most secure?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider how much of the baseline the three share, and what decides the rest for your team.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== None of them, and all of them
layout: answer
section: faq
Baseline capabilities are 80% equivalent. The 20% delta matters for specific workloads; for most commercial workloads, any of the three is well past the bar. The most secure cloud for you is the one your team operates most fluently.
notes:
None of them, and all of them. Baseline capabilities are 80% equivalent. The 20% delta matters for specific workloads, such as regulated workloads, Zero-Trust-shaped architectures and Microsoft-stack shops, but for most commercial workloads any of the three is well past the bar.

The page's answer is that the cloud that's most secure for you is the one your team operates with the most fluency.

== Is multi-cloud more or less secure than single-cloud?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider what a second or third cloud does to the surface your security team has to operate.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Less, at the same headcount
layout: answer
section: faq
Multi-cloud doubles or triples the surface area your team has to operate. It's a fine strategic outcome when it serves real business drivers, but it should not be picked for its own sake.
notes:
Less, at the same headcount. Multi-cloud doubles or triples the surface area your team has to operate.

The page says the multi-cloud for resilience argument rarely survives contact with reality, because the providers themselves are far more reliable than a multi-cloud failover engineered by humans. Multi-cloud is a fine strategic outcome when it serves real business drivers, such as acquired companies, regulatory requirements or customer demand, but it should not be picked for its own sake.
